        "raw": "**Short answer**\n\n| OS | Command | What it prints |\n|----|---------|----------------|\n| **Linux / macOS** | `find /path/to/dir -type f` | Every file’s absolute path |\n| **Windows CMD** | `dir /s /b /a-d C:\\path\\to\\dir` | Every file’s full path |\n| **Windows PowerShell** | `Get-ChildItem -Recurse -File | Select-Object -ExpandProperty FullName` | Every file’s full path |\n| **Python (cross‑platform)** | `import os; [os.path.join(r, f) for r, d, f in os.walk('/path/to/dir')]` | List of full paths |\n\n---\n\n## Why these are the “best” ways\n\n| Feature | Why it matters |\n|---------|----------------|\n| **Recursion** | Handles any depth of sub‑directories automatically. |\n| **Full path** | No need to prepend the base directory later. |\n| **Speed** | Native OS utilities (`find`, `dir`, `Get‑ChildItem`) are highly optimised. |\n| **Simplicity** | One line, no extra scripting. |\n| **Cross‑platform** | The Python snippet works everywhere. |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Unix‑like systems (Linux, macOS, WSL)\n\n```bash\n# Absolute paths, all files\nfind /path/to/dir -type f\n```\n\n* `-type f` limits the output to regular files (skips directories, symlinks, etc.).\n* If you want to include symlinks that point to files, drop the `-type f` or add `-follow`.\n\n**Example**\n\n```bash\n$ find /tmp/example -type f\n/tmp/example/file1.txt\n/tmp/example/subdir/file2.txt\n/tmp/example/subdir/another/file3.log\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Windows Command Prompt\n\n```cmd\ndir /s /b /a-d C:\\path\\to\\dir\n```\n\n* `/s` – recurse into sub‑directories.  \n* `/b` – use the bare format (just the path).  \n* `/a-d` – exclude directories (`-d` means “do not list directories”).\n\n**Example**\n\n```cmd\nC:\\> dir /s /b /a-d C:\\example\nC:\\example\\file1.txt\nC:\\example\\subdir\\file2.txt\nC:\\example\\subdir\\another\\file3.log\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Windows PowerShell\n\n```powershell\nGet-ChildItem -Recurse -File | Select-Object -ExpandProperty FullName\n```\n\n* `-Recurse` – walk all sub‑folders.  \n* `-File` – only files (skip directories).  \n* `Select-Object -ExpandProperty FullName` – output just the full path string.\n\n**Example**\n\n```powershell\nPS C:\\> Get-ChildItem -Recurse -File | Select-Object -ExpandProperty FullName\nC:\\example\\file1.txt\nC:\\example\\subdir\\file2.txt\nC:\\example\\subdir\\another\\file3.log\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Cross‑platform Python snippet\n\n```python\nimport os\n\nroot = '/path/to/dir'          # change this\nfiles = [os.path.join(r, f)\n         for r, d, f in os.walk(root)\n         for f in f]\nprint('\\n'.join(files))\n```\n\n* `os.walk()` yields `(root, dirs, files)` tuples for every directory.  \n* `os.path.join()` builds the absolute path.  \n* The list comprehension flattens the nested structure into a single list.\n\n**Run it**\n\n```bash\n$ python3 list_files.py\n/path/to/dir/file1.txt\n/path/to/dir/subdir/file2.txt\n/path/to/dir/subdir/another/file3.log\n```\n\n---\n\n## Bonus: If you need the list in a file\n\n| OS | Command | Redirect |\n|----|---------|----------|\n| **Linux / macOS** | `find /path/to/dir -type f > filelist.txt` | `>` |\n| **Windows CMD** | `dir /s /b /a-d C:\\path\\to\\dir > filelist.txt` | `>` |\n| **PowerShell** | `Get-ChildItem -Recurse -File | Select-Object -ExpandProperty FullName > filelist.txt` | `>` |\n| **Python** | `with open('filelist.txt', 'w') as f: f.write('\\n'.join(files))` | `open()` |\n\n---\n\n### TL;DR\n\n- **Linux/macOS**: `find /path/to/dir -type f`\n- **Windows CMD**: `dir /s /b /a-d C:\\path\\to\\dir`\n- **Windows PowerShell**: `Get-ChildItem -Recurse -File | Select-Object -ExpandProperty FullName`\n- **Python**: `os.walk()` + `os.path.join()`\n\nPick the one that matches your environment and you’ll get a full list of every file in the directory tree."
